= What does a definite decision establish?

A robot remembers a can on a shelf. On its next visit a white panel covers the place where the can sat. Deleting the record may be correct because a person took the can away. It may also be a mistake because someone put the panel in front of it. A benchmark with access to the scene state can tell these stories apart. The robot may receive precisely the same observation under either story.

We study one decision in a memory system. Its input is an earlier color image, a tight box around a remembered object in that image, and one later color image. Its output is *present*, *removed*, or *unknown*. “Removed” concerns the remembered place. The robot has no access to the intervening events. A temporal prior could inform its guess, and another view could change its evidence. This experiment fixes the observation and asks what a decision about that place can establish.

#figure(image("figures/counterfactual-example.png",width:100%), caption:[Two examples from the exploratory MuJoCo cohort. The yellow outline identifies the remembered tuna can. The table pair changes 12,159 pixels in the full revisit. The two shelf revisits are identical in all 810,000 pixels. Each verifier receives only one revisit. Crops are for display.]) <example>

The comparison in @example motivated a test on fresh arrangements. A method that always abstains passes every ambiguity check, and a method that always reports removal passes every clear-removal check. The audit puts those outcomes side by side. It also records false removals, including cases in which moving a camera makes a retained object appear absent.

= Relation to prior work

SceneDiff annotates and predicts verifiable object changes across visits with different viewpoints. STILL registers a hypothesized object's former place with estimated geometry and asks a trained vision-language model whether the object remains there. It includes an unseen response, hard same-capture controls, and a two-view evaluation. Its learned verifier combines scene and local-place evidence. These studies already establish both the problem of occlusion and the value of registering a place. Our experiment starts with a specified object and an earlier reference box, changes its physical state in a controlled pair, and counts how often a verifier makes a definite decision on *identical* inputs. The estimand is conditional on that interface and on a known target, whereas detection average precision also reflects proposal generation, ranking, and localization. @scenediff @still

SceneForge derives paired observations and effect-aware annotations by intervening in a 3D world. Its shadows and reflections illustrate why an invisible target mesh can still affect RGB. VA-TAMP gathers new observations before executing actions that depend on uncertain object models. Our experiment holds the viewpoint budget to one revisit so that a failure can be attributed to the information available at a particular memory update. Coverage and conditional error are standard in selective classification. Here a matched alternate world lets the evaluator certify a subset of unsupported decisions directly. @sceneforge @vatamp @reject

PBD-AG already tests false absence in robot memory. It projects a stored 3D object into measured depth and requires repeated observable misses before removing it. Disabling that visibility check increases false absence in its dynamic evaluation. SafeVantage uses evidence from reachable viewpoints to answer or abstain on category-presence queries under a travel budget. A pair in our audit instead specifies one previously seen object and fixes the reference, task and revisit pose. It tests the decision from that particular observation without access to depth or further exploration. @pbdag @safevantage

Matched observations also have a history in object-permanence research. IntPhys constructs sets of videos from matched frames and varies their temporal order to test physical plausibility. HuMeGo tests actions that require an earlier cue after it leaves view. In our audit, even the ordered reference and revisit can coincide across opposite target states. BAVO-Bench varies the camera at the same scene state to measure visibility gained by moving. We make the complementary intervention of varying the target state at the same camera pose. @intphys @humego @bavo

TRAPSBench pairs answerable and unanswerable physics questions to test selective abstention. The Counterfactual Memory Audit varies the available history while holding the current observation identical and measures whether the action follows the history. Our exact pairs hold the supplied history fixed as well. The hidden object's physical state changes, so the same removal answer necessarily deletes one retained object in that pair. @trapsbench @cma

SpatialUncertain derives labels from scene geometry. Human reviewers screen questions about partially occluded views for answerability. A separate perspective task tests whether another viewpoint resolves apparent size ambiguity. VAD-R studies spontaneous abstention on image questions and finds that a model can encode answerability while still answering. Our verifier receives an explicit UNKNOWN option. We measure whether a specified memory update follows from a complete two-image input that can recur under either physical state. @spatialuncertain @vad-r

= Counterfactual audit

== Two physical states, one input

Let $x = (r,b,q,t)$ comprise the decoded reference image, its numeric box, the decoded query image, and the fixed task. For a given engine, layout, blocker, camera stop and motion, generate $x_P$ with the target retained and $x_R$ with the target removed. The two states are equally represented by construction. An exact certificate requires equality of every component delivered to the method. File metadata, case names and pairing information are excluded from the method interface. We compare both entire decoded images at inference resolution and check the native rasters separately. Each member of a matched pair goes through inference independently, including pairs with identical inputs.

If $x_P = x_R$, a deterministic verifier must return the same answer on both. A definite answer then yields exactly one physical success and one physical error. Both commitments lack information that identifies which of the two interventions occurred. Abstention yields zero physical successes and zero wrong commitments. Across $c$ exact pairs with a definite answer, the observations therefore establish $2c$ unsupported decisions and $c$ physical errors. When $c > 0$, the physical error rate among these commitments is exactly one half under the balanced intervention design. Additional unsupported decisions may exist on unequal observations. When an engine yields no exact pair, this test supplies no conditional rate for that engine.

== Paired scenes and camera revisits

The exploratory cohort comprised ten layouts, five per environment, with three motion magnitudes and five camera stops. It informed the choice of comparators and endpoints. We kept its estimates separate. The earlier dense adapter failed its development stop rule. We retained that fixed adapter as a failure case for measurement on new layouts. Local protocol records place the design, settings and primary criteria before generation of these layouts. Protocol and results first entered public Git history together.

The new panel crosses a shelf and a tabletop, two target identities (a gelatin box and a large marker), two blocker families, and two independently seeded layouts per combination. Neither object served as a target in the earlier ten-layout comparison. Each layout uses five camera stops and one lighting setting. The wide panel favors severe occlusion. The white box allows the exposed fraction to change with the camera stop. At each stop a clear, retained nominal reference is paired with all four combinations of blocker parked/placed and target retained/removed. Queries use the nominal camera or a 6 cm horizontal shift with a 3° yaw. The signs of translation and rotation cycle across layouts and stops. The same object meshes, transforms and camera specifications go to both engines. A new lighting environment, target category or camera trajectory would constitute a different experiment. @ycb @posecnn @isaac @mujoco

The intervention encodes removal in a static scene by disabling the target’s render geometry. Isaac Sim toggles USD visibility. MuJoCo switches the target geoms to an excluded rendering group. Both retain the object definition and its nominal transform. The physical-state label used below denotes the target’s presence in the rendered scene, checked against the enabled flag and instance masks. We use “clear” as shorthand for the parked-blocker condition. That intervention leaves other objects in place. Contact forces, object motion during removal and subsequent settling are outside this experiment.

Each layout starts with its own block of 32 reserved development seeds. The generator accepts the first arrangement satisfying its existing geometric, frame-margin and duplicate checks. Feasibility trials on separate seeds selected objects that fit both scenes. Pudding-box trials had failed on the shelf before collection. This fixed two-object panel therefore measures transfer to these feasible targets. Isaac Sim 6.2.0.0 uses the RTX renderer. MuJoCo 3.17.0 uses Mesa llvmpipe software rendering. The engines render at 2304 × 1296. A Lanczos resize supplies 1200 × 675 RGB to each scorer. The numeric reference box encloses the scaled half-open native bounds. The random case identifiers, interventions, instance masks, camera parameters and registration ground truth remain outside the inference call.

== Verifiers and fixed decision rules

The *homography* baseline aligns image edges using a coarse-to-fine enhanced correlation coefficient fit that excludes the target neighborhood. It rejects implausible fits, including a shift exceeding 200 pixels at the delivered resolution. The *dense geometry* adapter estimates camera pose and depth from the two RGB images using VGGT. It projects each reference location into the query and filters out-of-frame, invalid and depth-inconsistent correspondences using an adaptation of GeoSCD's geometry stage. It abstains when less than 60% of the reference box has a usable map. This comparison changes registration while keeping the downstream evidence and all cutoffs fixed. Both methods infer registration from RGB alone. @ecc @geoscd @vggt

After registration, DINOv2 patch similarities measure target appearance. Local quality combines image checks, and the obstruction score uses object detections and foreground/background appearance changes. With acceptable registration and quality at least 0.30, a match of at least 0.70 yields PRESENT. A match at most 0.60 and obstruction at most 0.40 yields REMOVED. All other cases yield UNKNOWN. These thresholds came from earlier development. The experiment measures the behavior at these fixed operating points. @dinov2 @yoloworld

We also specified a zero-shot registered-place adaptation using Qwen3.5-9B. It applies the VGGT registration gate before calling the language model. The scorer marked the reference box and resized both full images to 768 × 432. It supplied separate reference and warped revisit crops at up to 512 × 512. Although the prompt described the reference crop as enlarged, the crop routine only downsampled oversized crops before the model processor. Black pixels indicate an unavailable mapping. The prompt requires visual evidence at the remembered place for either definite answer. The scorer uses greedy decoding and accepts one of the three literal responses. This adaptation differs from STILL's trained verifier, geometry and score fusion. The prespecified capability screen required at least 34/36 correct same-view answers and 36/40 per clear class on archived inputs. It included no hidden cases. We report this arm as a diagnostic of the tested prompt and image preparation. @still @qwen

== Calibration and statistical unit

Four separate fixed-camera layouts contain a tuna can or foam brick and a side-offset blocker. Each intervention has a base image, two fresh repeats and a distractor-only change. An evaluator selects a 7 × 7 patch deep inside the intersection of the reference target and exposed retained-target masks. At least 25 pixels must qualify. The mean absolute RGB difference over these pixels must be at least 8 on the 0–255 scale and exceed the larger first-repeat difference by at least 4. Second repeats and distractor manipulations test false triggers in each physical state. Any trigger fails the screen. Positive yield must reach eight of ten clear pairs in each engine and environment and include one partly visible placed pair in every layout. A positive witness measures localized image change. Semantic recognition of an object or a vacancy requires different validation.

The primary comparison is the difference between methods in correct removal decisions with the blocker parked at 6 cm and 3°, divided by *all* such queries. We also report exact-pair commitments and overall coverage, correct answers, false removals and false presences. A case counts as a gain when dense geometry alone is correct and a loss when the homography alone is correct. Descriptive 95% percentile intervals resample complete layouts 10,000 times within shelf and tabletop strata. The same draw retains all camera stops, physical states, methods and engines. The target and blocker mixture can vary between draws within an environment. Engine renders of a layout are paired observations. With two layout replicates per target–blocker cell, these intervals describe variation within the chosen panel.

We specified four conditions for calling the registration change an improvement without an observed tradeoff. It must improve the primary endpoint in each engine, gain correct decisions on balance in at least two tabletop layouts per engine, introduce zero newly wrong definite answers relative to homography across the full panel, and abstain on every certified pair. An engine that produces no certified pairs leaves the last condition unassessed there. We report each condition independently. The protocol listed the registered language model as secondary. We chose the shared-registration and visibility breakdowns after inspecting the main results.
